# Systems Reviewer — v001
Read _committee_common_v001.md + _shared/TOOLING_v001.md.
MANDATE: is this a systems INSIGHT/DESIGN, or mere engineering integration? Search OSS (vLLM/SGLang/
LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM/etc.) for whether existing systems already implement this mechanism.
CHECK: does a shipping system already do this; is the contribution an insight or just glue; does it hold
at realistic scale/workload; reproducibility of the artifact.
OUTPUT:
```
ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green|yellow|red
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [{system, url, overlap}]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight | design | mechanism | integration-only
SCALE_REALISM: <holds at realistic workload? gaps?>
RATIONALE: <cite>
```
# Hostile Committee — common rules (v001)
You are ONE reviewer in a 6-member hostile committee. You review a COMPRESSED review packet (claim,
stage, hypothesis, evidence, negative evidence, closest prior work, mandatory baselines, artifacts,
previous verdict summary, decision requested) — NOT long session logs. You have an INDEPENDENT context.

RULES (all members):
- Source of truth = the packet's cited evidence + your own prior-art search via prompts/_shared/TOOLING_v001.md.
- ANTI-COPING: "novel/promising/significant/structural/impactful" FORBIDDEN unless immediately followed by a
  cited metric or named prior work (with retrievable URL/citation).
- Reason ONLY from evidence. If evidence is missing, say "insufficient evidence", do not assume.
- Hostile, not cynical: a clean negative result or honest YELLOW is a valid, valuable outcome.
- Output your vote in the structured block your role defines; the Area Chair aggregates. Do NOT mutate the
  academic map directly — propose map deltas.
- Record the prompt version you ran (v001) so verdicts are auditable.
VOTE VALUES: green | yellow | red  (+ your role-specific findings). Consensus: GREEN=all green; any yellow
(no red)=yellow; any red=red.

=== REVIEW PACKET ===
# COMMITTEE#1 — CLAIM-0063 (PROJ-0033, agentic-systems x data-systems/serialization)
## EMPIRICAL CROSS-AREA PHENOMENON, effect=PARTIAL-SUPPORT (scoped; one sub-leg HONESTLY KILLED by the researcher). Vote HONESTLY by role. Real 6/6 by role; committee#1 = gate to L1 or converge.

## CLAIM (as scoped after L0): In the standard JS/TS LLM-agent tool stack (Vercel AI SDK -> secure-json-parse -> Zod), a tool-call argument that is a JSON integer literal > 2^53 validated by the DEFAULT z.number() pattern is silently rounded to the nearest IEEE-754 double AND passes validation unflagged -> the tool gets a corrupted 64-bit ID, zero error surfaced. The seam: the runtime's TWO deployed defenses (secure-json-parse for prototype-pollution; Zod schema validation) are JOINTLY BLIND to numeric width for z.number(), and the loss is structurally UNRECOVERABLE post-parse (precision gone before any reviver/refine runs). NOT "JS loses bigints" (folklore) — the contribution is the joint-blindness of two NAMED production safety layers + post-parse unrecoverability + a Python negative control isolating it to the JS runtime.

## ★ HONEST SCOPING (the researcher KILLED an over-broad sub-leg — credit this):
The original claim said "even z.number().int() is blind." MEASURED FALSE at current Zod: z.number().int() runs Number.isSafeInteger and REJECTS unsafe ints (silent_pass=0, 0/6509). The researcher DROPPED this sub-leg honestly. The SURVIVING claim is scoped to the DEFAULT z.number() integer tool-arg pattern.

## L0 RESULT (EXP-0076; N=11,009 probes incl 9 real public snowflakes + random [2^53,2^63) + control [0,2^53); pinned current versions)
PINNED (verified in installed source, 2026-06-04): ai 6.0.196 (NOTE: current major is v6, vendors secure-json-parse inside @ai-sdk/provider-utils 4.0.27), zod 4.4.3, secure-json-parse 4.1.0, lossless-json 4.3.0, node 22.22.3.
VERIFIED CODE PATH (real, not reimplemented): ai parse-tool-call.ts doParseToolCall -> safeParseJSON({text, schema}) (the EXACT exported fns); secure-json-parse._parse = native JSON.parse (float64) + prototype-pollution scan only; Zod v4 z.number() has NO safe-int guard (OPEN), z.number().int() runs Number.isSafeInteger (CLOSED).
- z.number() (DEFAULT pattern) — SEAM OPEN: silent_pass = 1.0 (6231/6231 corrupted accepted by Zod, zero error); realistic random-ID survival = 0.0037 (<0.01 threshold); CONTROL band [0,2^53) survival = 1.0.
- z.number().int() — HONEST KILL of that sub-leg: rejects 0/6509 unsafe -> silent_pass=0.
CONTROLS: Python negative control HELD (json.loads parse_int=int + Pydantic 2.13.4 survive 1.0 both bands -> corruption is the JS runtime number model, NOT the LLM/schema concept). Safe-band control = 1.0.
UNRECOVERABILITY DEMONSTRATED: for 1234567890123456789, .refine(Number.isSafeInteger) rejects but cannot recover; JSON.parse reviver sees the already-rounded float; only lossless-json (parse-time) recovers exactly -- and it is NOT the AI SDK default. Structurally unrecoverable post-parse.
PREVALENCE (public, deterministic): APIs.guru 150 public APIs sampled, 21.3% declare >=1 int64 field; 2.82% of all integer fields are int64; snowflakes + ns-timestamps always >2^53.

## KILLER SCREEN (researcher's L0, for your scrutiny): #1 not metric-validity (bit-exact corruption). #2/#3 NOT folklore: the seam = two NAMED defenses jointly blind + post-parse-unrecoverable + Python negative control = the irreducible contribution; mechanism = IEEE-754/ECMA-262 (no named-quantity paper). #4 currency=correctness (survival+silent_pass), bound 2^53 same currency. #5 runs ACTUAL AI SDK + Zod path. #6 the standard mitigation IS the two layers modeled, run as-shipped; real fix (lossless parser / .refine(isSafeInteger)) unimplemented-by-default. #7 baseline = recommended AI SDK + z.number() tool def, verified current source. #8 L0 CAN fail (the .int() sub-leg DID fail -> killed honestly). #9 prod-framework prior-art current releases: AI SDK v6 + secure-json-parse + Zod v4 checked, default z.number() leaves seam OPEN; lossless-json exists but not default/not wired. #10 public-data measurable (APIs.guru, snowflakes), no traffic constant. #11 version-chase cleared (core = IEEE-754/ECMA-262 unmovable; tools verified at current release).

## ★ THE STRESS-TESTS committee#1 SHOULD PROBE:
1. Is the surviving scoped claim (z.number() default) the REAL production default, or is z.number().int() / z.int() the recommended pattern for ID fields (which WOULD close it)? i.e. does mcp-to-ai-sdk / the AI SDK docs generate z.number() or z.number().int() for an integer/int64 schema field? If the recommended/codegen default for an int64 ID is .int() (closed), the open-seam shrinks to "developers who hand-write z.number() for an ID" -> weaken.
2. Is the killer-#3 framing sufficient? Two named defenses jointly blind + unrecoverable + Python-isolated -- is that a publishable seam, or "known JS bigint loss, re-scoped"? (Stronger than CLAIM-0061's: TWO defenses + structural unrecoverability + a clean cross-language control. But still rests on a known number-model limit.)
3. Does the corruption reach the TOOL with real consequence, or do downstream layers (DB driver, API client) re-stringify/catch it? The claim needs the corrupted id to actually mis-execute.

## ORCHESTRATOR NOTE (NOT advocacy): clean, honestly-scoped L0 (a sub-leg self-killed; controls held; current versions verified -> cleared the version-chase trap that killed CLAIM-0062). The surviving z.number()-default seam is real (silent_pass=1.0, unrecoverable, Python-isolated). The live questions are (1) whether z.number() (vs .int()) is the real production default for ID fields, and (2) the killer-#3 altitude. If the committee judges the seam open at the real default + the framing publishable -> advance to an L1 (codegen-default audit + end-to-end tool-execution consequence + broader schema corpus). If z.number().int() is the recommended default for IDs -> weaken. Judge honestly. Real 6/6 by role. Prompt version v003.
=== END PACKET ===
