3:vLLM currently supports named function calling, as well as the `auto`, `required` (as of `vllm>=0.8.3`), and `none` options for the `tool_choice` field in the chat completion API.
7:Start the server with tool calling enabled. This example uses Meta's Llama 3.1 8B model, so we need to use the `llama3_json` tool calling chat template from the vLLM examples directory:
11:    --enable-auto-tool-choice \
12:    --tool-call-parser llama3_json \
13:    --chat-template examples/tool_chat_template_llama3.1_json.jinja
16:Next, make a request that triggers the model to use the available tools:
22:    import json
28:    tool_functions = {"get_weather": get_weather}
30:    tools = [
51:        tools=tools,
52:        tool_choice="auto",
55:    tool_call = response.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0].function
56:    print(f"Function called: {tool_call.name}")
57:    print(f"Arguments: {tool_call.arguments}")
58:    print(f"Result: {tool_functions[tool_call.name](**json.loads(tool_call.arguments))}")
71:* Setting up the server with tool calling enabled
72:* Defining an actual function to handle tool calls
73:* Making a request with `tool_choice="auto"`
76:You can also specify a particular function using named function calling by setting `tool_choice={"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_weather"}}`. Note that this will use the structured outputs backend - so the first time this is used, there will be several seconds of latency (or more) as the FSM is compiled for the first time before it is cached for subsequent requests.
80:1. Define appropriate tools in the request
82:3. Handle the tool calls in your application logic
84:For more advanced usage, including parallel tool calls and different model-specific parsers, see the sections below.
91:vLLM will use structured outputs to ensure the response matches the tool parameter object defined by the JSON schema in the `tools` parameter.
92:For best results, we recommend ensuring that the expected output format / schema is specified in the prompt to ensure that the model's intended generation is aligned with the schema that it's being forced to generate by the structured outputs backend.
94:To use a named function, you need to define the functions in the `tools` parameter of the chat completion request, and
95:specify the `name` of one of the tools in the `tool_choice` parameter of the chat completion request.
99:vLLM supports the `tool_choice='required'` option in the chat completion API. Similar to the named function calling, it also uses structured outputs, so this is enabled by default and will work with any supported model. However, support for alternative decoding backends are on the [roadmap](../usage/v1_guide.md#features) for the V1 engine.
101:When tool_choice='required' is set, the model is guaranteed to generate one or more tool calls based on the specified tool list in the `tools` parameter. The number of tool calls depends on the user's query. The output format strictly follows the schema defined in the `tools` parameter.
105:vLLM supports the `tool_choice='none'` option in the chat completion API. When this option is set, the model will not generate any tool calls and will respond with regular text content only, even if tools are defined in the request.
108:    When tools are specified in the request, vLLM includes tool definitions in the prompt by default, regardless of the `tool_choice` setting. To exclude tool definitions when `tool_choice='none'`, use the `--exclude-tools-when-tool-choice-none` option.
112:Whether vLLM enforces the tool parameter schema during generation depends on the `tool_choice` mode:
114:| `tool_choice` value | Schema-constrained decoding | Behavior |
116:| Named function | Yes (via structured outputs backend) | Arguments are guaranteed to be valid JSON conforming to the function's parameter schema. |
117:| `"required"` | Yes (via structured outputs backend) | Same as named function. The model must produce at least one tool call. |
118:| `"auto"` | No | The model generates freely. A tool-call parser extracts tool calls from the raw text. Arguments may be malformed or not match the schema. |
119:| `"none"` | N/A | No tool calls are produced. |
121:When schema conformance matters, prefer `tool_choice="required"` or named function calling over `"auto"`.
125:The [OpenAI API](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/function-calling#strict-mode) supports a `strict` field on function definitions. When set to `true`, OpenAI uses constrained decoding to guarantee that tool-call arguments match the function schema, even in `tool_choice="auto"` mode.
127:vLLM **does not implement** `strict` mode today. The `strict` field is accepted in requests (to avoid breaking clients that set it), but it has no effect on decoding behavior. In auto mode, argument validity depends entirely on the model's output quality and the parser's extraction logic.
135:* `--enable-auto-tool-choice` -- **mandatory** Auto tool choice. It tells vLLM that you want to enable the model to generate its own tool calls when it
137:* `--tool-call-parser` -- select the tool parser to use (listed below). Additional tool parsers
138:will continue to be added in the future. You can also register your own tool parsers in the `--tool-parser-plugin`.
139:* `--tool-parser-plugin` -- **optional** tool parser plugin used to register user defined tool parsers into vllm, the registered tool parser name can be specified in `--tool-call-parser`.
140:* `--chat-template` -- **optional** for auto tool choice. It's the path to the chat template which handles `tool`-role messages and `assistant`-role messages
141:that contain previously generated tool calls. Hermes, Mistral and Llama models have tool-compatible chat templates in their
142:`tokenizer_config.json` files, but you can specify a custom template. This argument can be set to `tool_use` if your model has a tool use-specific chat
143:template configured in the `tokenizer_config.json`. In this case, it will be used per the `transformers` specification. More on this [here](https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/en/chat_templating#why-do-some-models-have-multiple-templates)
144:from HuggingFace; and you can find an example of this in a `tokenizer_config.json` [here](https://huggingface.co/NousResearch/Hermes-2-Pro-Llama-3-8B/blob/main/tokenizer_config.json).
146:If your favorite tool-calling model is not supported, please feel free to contribute a parser & tool use chat template!
149:    With `tool_choice="auto"`, tool-call arguments are extracted from the model's raw text output by the selected parser. No schema-level constraint is applied during decoding, so arguments may occasionally be malformed or violate the function's parameter schema. See [Constrained Decoding Behavior](#constrained-decoding-behavior) for details.
159:_Note that the Hermes 2 **Theta** models are known to have degraded tool call quality and capabilities due to the merge
